    "body": "# PR type\n\n- [x] Bug Fix\n- [ ] New Feature\n- [ ] Document Updates\n- [ ] More Models or Datasets Support\n\n# PR information\n\nRelated: #10116 (gradient-accumulation normalization).\n\nWhen dynamic rollout produces more samples than the per-device batch size, `_compute_loss_chunked()` takes a sample-weighted mean of chunk losses. DAPO/CISPO/FIPO policy losses are already normalized by the global completion-token count, so this applies an extra chunk sample weight. With gradient accumulation set to 1, splitting four samples into two or four chunks shrinks every policy gradient to 1/2 or 1/4. A 3+2 split weights the first three samples by 0.6 and the remaining two by 0.4.\n\nPass the inverse chunk sample weight into `_compute_loss_and_metrics()` and apply it only to the window-normalized policy loss, before SDAR/CHORD are added. The existing outer weighted mean then sums the policy contributions correctly while retaining the auxiliary-loss weighting. Dummy chunks use a scale of 1 and retain their zero-weight backward graph.\n\nThis contains only the production change, as requested by the submitter; additional regression tests remain local. Searches for GRPO/chunked loss and references to #10116 found no duplicate fix. #10116 addresses accumulation-step scaling; #10256 concerns the separate Liger path. This bug also occurs with accumulation set to 1.\n\nAI assistance: OpenAI Codex implemented the patch and ran the validation below. Human review/attestation checklist items remain unchecked.\n\n## Experiment results\n\nCPU; Python 3.12.13, PyTorch 2.13.0+cu130, Transformers 5.14.1, TRL 0.29.1. Reproductions use a small trainable output head and the actual trainer dispatch, slicing, loss reduction, and backward functions.\n\n| DAPO execution, accumulation=1 | Before | After | Gradient ratio after |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Unchunked | -0.333333 | -0.333333 | 1 |\n| Two equal chunks | -0.166667 | -0.333333 | 1 |\n| Four equal chunks | -0.083333 | -0.333333 | 1 |\n\nFor 3+2 samples, per-sample gradient ratios change from `[0.6, 0.6, 0.6, 0.4, 0.4]` to all ones. CISPO and FIPO behave equivalently. GRPO and Dr. GRPO controls retain matching results.\n\n```bash\nPYTHONPATH=\"$PWD\" OMP_NUM_THREADS=2 MKL_NUM_THREADS=2 .venv/bin/python -m pytest \\\n  /tmp/ms-swift-grpo-chunk-review/test_chunk_normalization.py \\\n  tests/test_align/test_grpo_loss.py \\\n  tests/train/test_grpo_reward_metrics.py -q\n```\n\n**113 passed**: 84 local regression cases and 29 existing tests. Coverage includes equal/unequal chunks, train/eval, accumulation 1/4, FP32/FP64, real CHORD SFT loss, SDAR at the loss-composition boundary, and dummy-chunk backward with simulated rank sizes. Of the initial 81 local cases, 51 chunking comparisons fail on the upstream base; the later three FP32 checks also pass after the fix. This is CPU correctness testing, not a full distributed rollout or pretrained-model quality evaluation.\n\nAlso ran the broader suite including `tests/rollout/test_server_exact_token_io.py`: **38 passed, 2 subtests passed, 2 failed, 1 setup error** on both the patch and the upstream baseline. The failures are unchanged fixtures: `ExactTokenTemplate` lacks `_get_response_prefix` in two cases, and the standalone Ray scheduler test lacks the `trainer_name` fixture.\n\n`pre-commit run --all-files`: all hooks passed. `git diff --check`: passed.\n\n# Checklist\n\n- [x] I have linked the related issue above, if there is one.\n- [ ] I have reviewed and understand every changed line, and I can explain how the change works and why it is correct.\n- [ ] I have run the relevant tests or reproductions locally, and included the commands and results above (if applicable).\n- [ ] I am responsible for the content of this PR, regardless of how it was produced.\n",
